commonMistakes:
  - title: "Dividing by the New Value"
    description: "Always divide by the original starting baseline (Old Value). Dividing by the new value gives the fraction of the final number, producing an incorrect percentage."
  - title: "Confusing Percentage Points with Percentage Change"
    description: "If tax rises from 10% to 12%, that is a 2 percentage point increase, but a 20% relative percentage increase ((12 - 10) / 10 = 0.20)."
  - title: "Assuming Inverse Symmetry"
    description: "A 50% drop requires a 100% gain to break even. If a $100 stock falls 50% to $50, gaining 50% only returns it to $75."
  - title: "Attempting to Calculate Change from Zero"
    description: "Division by zero is mathematically undefined. You cannot calculate relative percentage change from an initial baseline of zero."
